<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shenzhen</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shenzhen is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangdong">Guangdong</a> province in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, bordering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a>. In 1980 it became one of China's first Special Economic Zones under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deng-xiaoping">Deng Xiaoping</a>'s reforms, and it grew from a small market town into a metropolis of more than 17 million people, a centre of manufacturing, electronics and technology companies.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shenzhen.jpg" alt="Shenzhen" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Charlie fong · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Commercial_area_of_futian_to_east2020.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Shenzhen lies on the east bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pearl-river">Pearl River</a> estuary on the central coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangdong">Guangdong</a>. It borders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a> to the south, Dongguan to the north, Huizhou to the northeast and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macau">Macau</a> to the southwest. Although it sits about a degree south of the Tropic of Cancer, it has a warm, humid subtropical climate shaped by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monsoon">monsoon</a> and the Siberian anticyclone, with mild winters and a rainy season that lasts until late September or early October.</p><p>The city roughly follows the administrative boundaries of the historical Bao'an County. Its name means deep ditch in the local vernacular, from words used for irrigation ditches. The earliest recorded mention of the name dates from 1410, under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming dynasty</a>, but no market town was established there until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Humans have lived in the area for some 6,700 years, with the oldest evidence from the mid-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> period. Historic counties were first established there about 1,700 years ago, and the towns of Nantou and Dapeng were built over 600 years ago. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-opium-war">Second Opium War</a> the United Kingdom seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong-island">Hong Kong Island</a> and was leased the New Territories, which fixed the modern boundary between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a> and the area of Shenzhen, then a village on the border.</p><p>In 1953 the Bao'an County government moved to Shenzhen, which was closer to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kowloon">Kowloon</a>–Canton Railway and had a larger economy than Nantou. From the 1950s to the end of the 1970s the county received a large influx of refugees trying to reach <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a>; between 100,000 and 560,000 are said to have lived there. The place became a city by 1979. In May 1980 the Central Committee designated it the first special economic zone in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, as part of the reform and opening up promoted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deng-xiaoping">Deng Xiaoping</a>, an experiment in market <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitalism">capitalism</a> within a community guided by socialism with Chinese characteristics. It then had a population of 30,000.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Shenzhen is a prefecture-level city with nine districts and one new district. Its permanent population was recorded as 17,560,000 in 2020, many of them migrants from other parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, so that the population is younger than that of most Chinese cities. Before the zone was established the local communities spoke Cantonese or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hakka-people">Hakka</a>, and the main local cuisine is Cantonese.</p><p>The city is a centre of the technology and manufacturing industries, and is home to the Shenzhen Stock Exchange and companies such as Huawei, Tencent and DJI. Its port is one of the large container ports of the world. The Shenzhen Metro, a rapid transit system, serves the city, and the city is part of the Pearl River Delta Metropolitan Region together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Guangzhou</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macau">Macau</a>, among others.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Shenzhen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From 1978 the reforms of Deng Xiaoping restored family farming under the household responsibility system, opened Special Economic Zones such as <b>Shenzhen</b> in 1980 and gradually admitted private enterprise and foreign investment.</blockquote><a href="/empire/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
